HY='THE ŚAKRĀDI STUTI'
V=[
VP(C,'2',S('2'),'THE GODS BOW',
 sc('Indra and the gods bowing low before the Goddess, their bodies glowing with joy',FLOWERS(6)+DEVI(110,0,.7)+LION(110,140,.5)+''.join(f'<g transform="translate({30+k*40} {150}) rotate({-20 if k<2 else 20})">'+GOD(0,0,.5,*c)+'</g>' for k,c in enumerate([('#ffd60a','#c98b5a'),('#fff','#9fd3f0'),('#ff8c1a','#e0a800'),('#2a9d6f','#c98b5a')]))),
 'When that most valiant, evil-souled Mahiṣa and the army of the gods’ enemy had been slain by the Devī, Śakra (Indra) and the hosts of gods praised her with words, their necks and shoulders bent low in reverence, their bodies beautiful with the thrill of joy.',
 [('śakra-ādayaḥ','<b>Indra and the others</b>: hence “Śakrādi stuti”'),('praṇati-namra','<b>bent low in bowing</b>'),('praharṣa-pulaka','<b>the thrill of joy</b>, hair standing on end'),('tuṣṭuvuḥ','<b>they praised</b>')],
 '<b>The name of the hymn.</b> It is called the <b>Śakrādi stuti</b>, the hymn of “Śakra (Indra) and the others”. Its long, flowing metre (vasantatilakā, “ornament of spring”) is unlike the plain narrative verses: the gods sing in their best voice.',
 'Victory is followed <b>by praise</b>.'),
VP(C,'3',S('3'),'SHE WHO SPREAD THROUGH ALL',
 sc('The Goddess at the centre with threads of light running from her through the whole world, gods and sages bowing',GLOW(110,70,70)+DEVI(110,10,.65,False)+''.join(f'<path d="M110 70 L{110+100*math.cos(k*0.52):.0f} {80+70*math.sin(k*0.52):.0f}" stroke="#e0a800" stroke-width="1.5"/>' for k in range(12))),
 'To that Devī, who by her own power pervades this world, whose form is the sum of the powers of all the hosts of gods; to that Ambikā, worthy of worship by all gods and great sages, we bow in devotion. May she grant us all that is auspicious.',
 [('tatam','<b>spread out, pervaded</b>'),('ātma-śaktyā','<b>by her own power</b>'),('śakti-samūha-mūrti','<b>whose form is the sum of the powers</b>'),('vidadhātu śubhāni','<b>may she grant good things</b>')],
 '<b>Chapter 2 in one line.</b> “The sum of the powers of all the gods” describes her birth (Part 7). But the gods add: she pervades the world by <i>her own</i> power. Their light did not make her; it revealed her.',
 'The gods’ power <b>was always hers</b>.'),
VP(C,'4',S('4'),'BEYOND ALL DESCRIPTION',
 sc('Vishnu, Brahma and Shiva standing with open mouths, unable to describe the vast glowing Goddess',GLOW(110,60,74)+DEVI(110,0,.9,False)+GOD(40,150,.6,'#ffd60a','#3b6fd0')+BRAHMA(110,140,.5)+GOD(180,150,.6,'#fff','#9fd3f0')+T(110,24,'? ? ?',14)),
 'Whose matchless glory and strength not even the Lord Ananta (Viṣṇu), Brahmā and Hara (Śiva) are able to describe: may that Caṇḍikā set her mind on protecting the whole world and destroying the fear of evil.',
 [('prabhāvam atulam','<b>matchless glory</b>'),('ananta','<b>the endless one</b>: Viṣṇu'),('hara','<b>Śiva</b>'),('na alam','<b>not able</b>'),('aśubha-bhaya','<b>the fear of evil</b>')],
 '<b>What the gods ask for.</b> Not riches, not victory: they ask her to protect the world and destroy <i>fear</i>. Fear of evil is often worse than evil itself.',
 'Even the trinity <b>cannot describe her</b>.'),
VP(C,'5',S('5'),'FORTUNE IN THE HOMES OF THE GOOD',
 sc('Two houses side by side: a bright one with a lotus over its door, and a dark one with a broken pot; between them the Goddess',
  '<rect x="10" y="70" width="70" height="70" fill="#fff3b0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M6 72 L45 36 L84 72z" fill="#c0392b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+MOUNT('lotus',45,96,.6)+'<rect x="140" y="70" width="70" height="70" fill="#555" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M136 72 L175 36 L214 72z" fill="#333" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M164 120 q10 -14 20 0 l-4 10 h-12z" fill="#7a5a3a" stroke="#111"/>'+DEVI(110,20,.45,False)),
 'You who are yourself Śrī (fortune) in the homes of the virtuous, and Alakṣmī (misfortune) in the homes of the wicked; intelligence in the hearts of the wise; faith in the good; and modesty in the well-born: to you we bow. O Devī, protect the universe.',
 [('śrī · alakṣmī','<b>fortune · misfortune</b>'),('sukṛtinām bhavaneṣu','<b>in the homes of the virtuous</b>'),('kṛta-dhiyām buddhiḥ','<b>understanding in the wise</b>'),('śraddhā','<b>faith</b>'),('lajjā','<b>modesty, conscience</b>')],
 '<b>The most-quoted verse of the hymn.</b> The same Goddess is fortune for one and misfortune for another: not by whim, but as the <b>fruit of how they live</b>. She is also the faith in good people and the conscience in the well-raised. She works from inside us.',
 'She is <b>fortune for the good, ruin for the wicked</b>.'),
VP(C,'7',S('7'),'THE FIRST NATURE',
 sc('Three coloured strands, white, red and black, braiding together into the whole world, held in the Goddess’s hands',''.join(f'<path d="M{20+k*10} 20 q90 40 {80-k*10} 120" fill="none" stroke="{c}" stroke-width="6"/>' for k,c in enumerate(['#f5f5f5','#c0392b','#222']))+'<circle cx="150" cy="110" r="30" fill="#4aa3df" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M128 104 q22 -10 44 0" stroke="#2a9d6f" stroke-width="5" fill="none"/>'+DEVI(150,0,.35,False)+T(56,150,'SATTVA · RAJAS · TAMAS',9)),
 'You are the cause of all the worlds; though made of the three guṇas, you are not known even by Hari and Hara because of their limits; you are boundless. You are the refuge of all; this whole world is a part of you. You are the unmanifest, supreme, primal Prakṛti.',
 [('hetuḥ','<b>the cause</b>'),('tri-guṇā','<b>made of the three qualities</b>'),('apārā','<b>boundless</b>'),('aṃśa-bhūtam','<b>is a part (of you)</b>'),('avyākṛtā prakṛtiḥ','<b>unmanifest Nature</b>'),('ādyā','<b>the first</b>')],
 '<b>Philosophy in a hymn.</b> In Sāṅkhya thought, <i>Prakṛti</i> is the unmanifest source from which the whole world unfolds through the three guṇas. The gods say: that is you. The whole world is only “a part” of her.',
 'The whole world is <b>only a part of her</b>.'),
VP(C,'8',S('8'),'SVĀHĀ AND SVADHĀ',
 sc('A sacred fire with offerings going up to the gods marked Svaha, and rice balls offered to the ancestors marked Svadha',FIRE(70,120,1.0)+'<path d="M70 100 q10 -40 0 -70" fill="none" stroke="#e0a800" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>'+T(70,22,'SVĀHĀ → gods',10)+''.join(f'<circle cx="{150+k*16}" cy="128" r="7" fill="#fff" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(3))+T(166,22,'SVADHĀ → ancestors',10)+'<path d="M166 120 v-80" stroke="#9fd3f0" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>'),
 'O Devī, by uttering you, all the gods are satisfied in every sacrifice: you are Svāhā. And you are the cause of the satisfaction of the hosts of ancestors: that is why people utter you as Svadhā.',
 [('samudīraṇa','<b>uttering</b>'),('tṛpti','<b>satisfaction</b>'),('makha','<b>sacrifice</b>'),('pitṛ-gaṇa','<b>the ancestors</b>'),('svāhā · svadhā','<b>the cries of offering to gods · to ancestors</b>')],
 '<b>Echo of Brahmā’s hymn.</b> Brahmā began with “tvaṃ svāhā tvaṃ svadhā” (Part 6). The gods now explain why: every offering to gods and to ancestors reaches them through her. The words of ritual are her names.',
 'Every offering <b>travels through her</b>.'),
VP(C,'9–10',S('9','10'),'KNOWLEDGE THAT FREES',
 sc('A sage meditating with closed eyes while three scrolls labelled Rig, Yajur and Sama float above, joined to the Goddess’s glow',GLOW(110,40,40)+DEVI(110,0,.4,False)+SAGE(110,150,.9)+''.join(f'<rect x="{30+k*60}" y="80" width="40" height="22" rx="4" fill="#fff3b0" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(3))+T(50,95,'ṚK',9)+T(110,95,'YAJUS',9)+T(170,95,'SĀMA',9)),
 'You are the cause of liberation, of unthinkable great vows; you are studied by sages seeking freedom, who have mastered their senses and cast off every fault. You are the supreme knowledge, the blessed Devī. You are the soul of sound, the treasury of the pure Ṛk and Yajus hymns and of the Sāman, lovely with its sung words. O Devī, you are the three Vedas; you are livelihood for the world’s welfare; you destroy the deepest pain of all.',
 [('mukti-hetu','<b>cause of liberation</b>'),('mokṣa-arthibhiḥ','<b>by those seeking freedom</b>'),('vidyā paramā','<b>supreme knowledge</b>'),('śabda-ātmikā','<b>whose self is sound</b>'),('trayī','<b>the three Vedas</b>'),('vārtā','<b>livelihood</b>: farming, trade')],
 '<b>From the Vedas to the farm.</b> She is the highest knowledge sought by sages, the three Vedas, and also <i>vārtā</i>, plain livelihood. Spiritual and practical life are both in her care.',
 'She is the Vedas, <b>and our daily bread</b>.'),
VP(C,'11',S('11'),'MEDHĀ, DURGĀ, ŚRĪ, GAURĪ',
 sc('Four small panels: a lamp for wisdom, a boat crossing a stormy sea, Lakshmi on Vishnu’s chest, and Gauri with the crescent-moon god',
  '<rect x="4" y="4" width="104" height="74" fill="#fff3b0" stroke="#111"/><rect x="112" y="4" width="104" height="74" fill="#dfe9f6" stroke="#111"/><rect x="4" y="82" width="104" height="74" fill="#fde4e4" stroke="#111"/><rect x="112" y="82" width="104" height="74" fill="#e7f4ea" stroke="#111"/>'
  +'<path d="M46 50 q10 -24 20 0z" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'+T(56,72,'MEDHĀ',9)+'<path d="M112 56 q26 -10 52 0 t52 0" fill="none" stroke="#2a6ab0" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M146 48 h36 l-6 10 h-24z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111"/>'+T(164,72,'DURGĀ',9)+GOD(56,140,.6,'#ffd60a','#3b6fd0')+'<circle cx="56" cy="120" r="5" fill="#c0392b"/>'+T(56,152,'ŚRĪ',9)+'<path d="M150 106 a10 10 0 1 0 10 10" fill="none" stroke="#e0a800" stroke-width="3"/>'+T(164,152,'GAURĪ',9)),
 'O Devī, you are Medhā, intelligence that knows the essence of all scriptures; you are Durgā, the boat that crosses the hard ocean of worldly life, without attachment; you are Śrī, who alone dwells in the heart of Kaiṭabha’s enemy (Viṣṇu); you indeed are Gaurī, established with the moon-crested one (Śiva).',
 [('medhā','<b>intelligence</b>'),('durgā','<b>“hard to reach”</b>: the one who carries us across'),('bhava-sāgara-nau','<b>boat over the sea of becoming</b>'),('kaiṭabha-ari','<b>enemy of Kaiṭabha</b>: Viṣṇu (Part 6)'),('śaśi-mauli','<b>moon-crested</b>: Śiva')],
 '<b>One Goddess, every consort.</b> She is Sarasvatī-like wisdom, Durgā the rescuer, Lakṣmī on Viṣṇu’s chest and Gaurī beside Śiva. The text is clear: these are not rivals but one Devī. Note “Durgā” explained as a boat across the <i>durga</i>, the hard-to-cross sea.',
 'Wisdom, rescuer, Lakṣmī, Gaurī: <b>all one Devī</b>.'),
VP(C,'12–13',S('12','13'),'HER SMILE, HER FROWN',
 sc('Two faces of the Goddess: one smiling softly like the full moon, one frowning fiercely like the rising red moon',
  '<circle cx="60" cy="70" r="40" fill="#fff8c0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M44 84 q16 12 32 0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><circle cx="48" cy="62" r="3" fill="#111"/><circle cx="72" cy="62" r="3" fill="#111"/>'
  +'<circle cx="160" cy="70" r="40" fill="#e05a3a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M144 90 q16 -10 32 0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><path d="M140 54 l14 6 M180 54 l-14 6" stroke="#111" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="148" cy="64" r="3" fill="#111"/><circle cx="172" cy="64" r="3" fill="#111"/>'+T(60,134,'SMILING',10)+T(160,134,'ANGRY',10)),
 'It is most wonderful that Mahiṣāsura, seeing your face, gently smiling, spotless, like the full moon’s disc, lovely as the finest gold, still struck at it in anger. And more wonderful still, O Devī, that seeing your face angry, terrible with frowning brows, the colour of the rising moon, Mahiṣa did not give up his life at once. For who can live after seeing Death himself enraged?',
 [('īṣat-sa-hāsam','<b>gently smiling</b>'),('pūrṇa-candra-bimba','<b>full moon’s disc</b>'),('bhrukuṭī-karālam','<b>terrible with frowns</b>'),('udyat-śaśāṅka','<b>the rising moon</b>, red'),('kupita-antaka','<b>Death enraged</b>')],
 '<b>Two wonders.</b> The gods tease Mahiṣa: who attacks a face so lovely? And who survives a face so terrible? Both are her faces. The beauty and the terror are one being.',
 'Her smile and her frown <b>are both wonders</b>.'),
VP(C,'15',S('15'),'WHEN SHE IS PLEASED',
 sc('A happy family at their door: honoured by neighbours, a full granary, a lamp burning, the Goddess smiling above',DEVI(170,0,.4,False)+'<rect x="20" y="70" width="80" height="70" fill="#fff3b0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M16 72 L60 36 L104 72z" fill="#c0392b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+person(130,150,'#ff8c1a','#c98b5a',.6)+person(156,150,'#e05a8a','#c98b5a',.55)+person(180,150,'#2a9d6f','#c98b5a',.4)+'<path d="M50 120 q10 -16 20 0z" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'),
 'Those to whom you, the giver of prosperity, are always gracious: they are honoured in the land, theirs is wealth, theirs is fame; their righteousness never fails; blessed are they, with devoted children, servants and wives.',
 [('sammatāḥ','<b>honoured</b>'),('dhanāni','<b>wealth</b>'),('yaśāṃsi','<b>fame</b>'),('dharma-varga','<b>the range of right living</b>'),('abhyudaya-dā','<b>giver of prosperity</b>')],
 '<b>A family blessing.</b> Many households chant this verse for the home: honour, wealth, good name, right living and a loyal family. Note that <i>dharma</i> is listed among the blessings: goodness itself is her gift.',
 'Her grace shows as <b>honour, wealth and a good home</b>.'),
VP(C,'17',S('17'),'“DURGE SMṚTĀ”',
 sc('A frightened traveller in a storm calling out, and a calm person at home in prayer; the Goddess’s light reaches both',NIGHTSKY+'<path d="M20 30 l10 20 l-8 0 l10 22" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="3" fill="none"/>'+person(50,150,'#888','#c98b5a',.8)+person(170,150,'#ff8c1a','#c98b5a',.8)+DEVI(110,0,.5,False)+'<path d="M96 60 L56 110 M124 60 L164 110" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>'+T(50,156,'IN FEAR',9,'#fff')+T(170,156,'AT EASE',9,'#fff')),
 'O Durgā, remembered, you take away the fear of every creature; remembered by the untroubled, you give a most auspicious mind. Remover of poverty, pain and fear, who but you is always tender-hearted, ready to help all?',
 [('durge smṛtā','<b>O Durgā, when remembered</b>'),('bhīti','<b>fear</b>'),('svasthaiḥ','<b>by those at ease</b>'),('dāridrya-duḥkha-bhaya-hāriṇi','<b>remover of poverty, pain and fear</b>'),('ārdra-cittā','<b>tender-hearted</b>')],
 '<b>Remember her in bad times and good.</b> The verse has two halves: in danger, remembering her removes fear; in good times, it gives a good mind. It is one of the most chanted lines of the whole Devī Māhātmya.',
 'Remembered in fear, she removes it; <b>in peace, she blesses the mind</b>.'),
VP(C,'18–19',S('18','19'),'WHY SHE KILLS',
 sc('An asura struck by the Goddess’s weapon rising, purified, toward heaven as a small glowing figure',BATTLE+DEVI(160,10,.55)+SWORD(120,90,-60,.8)+ASURA(70,150,.8)+'<path d="M70 110 v-70" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>'+GLOW(70,34,14)+GOD(70,50,.35,'#fff','#ffd60a',False)),
 '“When these are slain the world will be happy; let them not go on sinning for long and earn hell; let them die in battle and go to heaven.” Surely with this thought, O Devī, you slay the enemies. Could you not turn all the asuras to ashes by a mere look? Yet you hurl weapons at them, so that even enemies, purified by your weapons, may reach the higher worlds. Such is your kindly intent, even toward the hostile.',
 [('narakāya cirāya','<b>for a long time in hell</b>'),('saṅgrāma-mṛtyu','<b>death in battle</b>'),('bhasma','<b>ashes</b>'),('śastra-pūtāḥ','<b>purified by weapons</b>'),('sādhvī matiḥ','<b>a kindly intent</b>')],
 '<b>Killing as grace.</b> The gods explain her violence: she could burn the asuras with a glance, but she uses weapons so that dying by her hand <i>purifies</i> them. Even her enemies are her children.',
 'Even her killing <b>is kindness to the enemy</b>.'),
VP(C,'21–22',S('21','22'),'MERCY AND FIERCENESS TOGETHER',
 sc('The Goddess with a sword in one hand and the other hand raised in blessing, a heart glowing in her chest',BATTLE+DEVI(110,0,.85,False)+SWORD(60,100,-20,1)+'<path d="M150 80 q10 -20 20 0 q-10 14 -10 14 q0 0 -10 -14z" fill="#e05a8a" stroke="#111"/>'+GLOW(110,90,16,'#e05a8a',.5)),
 'O Devī, your nature is to quell the conduct of the wicked; this form of yours is unthinkable and unequalled; your valour slays those who robbed the gods of their might; and thus you have shown mercy even to enemies. To what can this valour of yours be compared? Where is a form like yours, which strikes fear in foes yet is so charming? Mercy in the heart and harshness in battle are seen, O boon-giver, in you alone in the three worlds.',
 [('durvṛtta-śamana','<b>quelling the wicked</b>'),('atulya','<b>unequalled</b>'),('dayā','<b>mercy</b>'),('citte kṛpā','<b>compassion in the heart</b>'),('samara-niṣṭhuratā','<b>harshness in battle</b>'),('varade','<b>O giver of boons</b>')],
 '<b>The one-line summary of the Goddess.</b> “Compassion in the heart, ruthlessness in battle”: in her alone are both seen. A mother protecting her child is both. This pair runs through the whole text.',
 'Compassion in the heart, <b>ruthlessness in battle</b>.'),
VP(C,'24–27',S('24','25','26','27'),'THE PRAYER FOR PROTECTION',
 sc('The Goddess at the centre of a compass, her trident, sword, bell and bow pointing to east, west, south and north',
  '<circle cx="110" cy="80" r="70" fill="#fff8ec" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M110 10 v140 M40 80 h140" stroke="#ccc" stroke-width="2"/>'+DEVI(110,30,.45,False)+TRIDENT(110,22,.5)+SWORD(176,90,90,.6)+'<path d="M42 70 q8 -18 16 0z" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111"/>'+'<path d="M104 130 q-14 14 0 28" fill="none" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="3"/>'+T(110,10,'N',10)+T(110,158,'S',10)+T(196,84,'E',10)+T(24,84,'W',10)),
 'Protect us with your trident, O Devī; protect us with your sword, O Ambikā; protect us with the sound of your bell and the twang of your bowstring. O Caṇḍikā, guard us in the east and in the west; guard us in the south; and in the north, O Īśvarī, by the whirling of your trident. With your gentle forms that move in the three worlds, and with your exceedingly terrible ones, protect us and the earth. O Ambikā, with the sword, trident, mace and all the weapons that rest in your sprout-soft hands, protect us on every side.',
 [('pāhi · rakṣa','<b>protect · guard</b>'),('ghaṇṭā-svana','<b>the sound of the bell</b>'),('prācī · pratīcī','<b>east · west</b>'),('saumyāni · ghorāṇi','<b>gentle forms · terrible forms</b>'),('kara-pallava','<b>hands soft as sprouts</b>')],
 '<b>The best-known lines.</b> “Śūlena pāhi no devi” is chanted daily by many as a protection prayer. Note the change of metre: the long verses give way to short, strong lines, easy to remember. Gentle and terrible forms are both asked to protect.',
 '“Śūlena pāhi no devi”: <b>guard us on every side</b>.'),
VP(C,'34–37','bhagavatyā kṛtaṁ sarvaṁ na kiñcidavaśiṣyate | / yadayaṁ nihataḥ śatrurasmākaṁ mahiṣāsuraḥ || 34|| / yadi cāpi varo deyastvayāsmākaṁ maheśvari | / saṁsmṛtā saṁsmṛtā tvaṁ no hiṁsethāḥ paramāpadaḥ || 35|| / yaśca martyaḥ stavairebhistvāṁ stoṣyatyamalānane | / tasya vittarddhivibhavairdhanadārādisampadām || 36|| / vṛddhaye’smatprasannā tvaṁ bhavethāḥ sarvadāmbike || 37||',
 'THE GODS’ BOON',
 sc('The Goddess smiling and offering a boon to the gods, who answer with folded hands; a mortal far below chanting from a book',FLOWERS(6)+DEVI(110,0,.6)+GODS(120,.4,4,50,40)+person(110,156,'#ff8c1a','#c98b5a',.5)+'<rect x="122" y="140" width="18" height="12" fill="#fff3b0" stroke="#111"/>'),
 'The gods said: “The Blessed One has done everything; nothing remains, since our enemy Mahiṣāsura is slain. Yet if a boon is to be given to us, O Great Ruler: whenever we remember you, again and again, destroy our worst dangers. And whatever mortal praises you with these hymns, O pure-faced one, may you, pleased with us, always be there for the growth of his wealth, prosperity and fortune, his riches, wife and blessings, O Ambikā.”',
 [('na kiñcit avaśiṣyate','<b>nothing remains to be done</b>'),('saṃsmṛtā saṃsmṛtā','<b>remembered again and again</b>'),('param āpadaḥ','<b>the worst dangers</b>'),('martyaḥ','<b>a mortal</b>'),('stavaiḥ ebhiḥ','<b>with these hymns</b>')],
 '<b>A boon for us.</b> The gods ask nothing for themselves except help whenever they remember her. Then they ask for <i>us</i>: any human who chants this hymn. That is why people chant it to this day.',
 'The gods’ boon is <b>for everyone who chants this hymn</b>.'),
]
for v in V: v['lab']+=' · '+HY if v['n']!='34–37' else ''
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="Indra on his white elephant and the other gods bowing before the Goddess, who stands smiling on her lion, flowers falling from the sky">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#3a1414" rx="8"/>'+''.join(f'<text x="{(37*k)%370+5}" y="{(23*k)%90+14}" font-size="12">🌸</text>' for k in range(22))+GLOW(190,100,90)+DEVI(190,20,.95)+LION(190,210,.75)
 +ELEPHANT(60,240,1.0)+GOD(60,206,.7,'#ffd60a','#c98b5a')+''.join(GOD(290+k*30,250,.6,*c) for k,c in enumerate([('#fff','#9fd3f0'),('#ff8c1a','#e0a800'),('#2a9d6f','#c98b5a')]))+'</svg>')
st=storypage('vanish','THE BOON AND THE PROMISE','Ślokas 4.28–42',
 panels('Three panels: the gods worshipping with flowers and incense; the Goddess granting the boon; the Goddess vanishing, with a glimpse of a fair goddess ahead',[
  ('#fde4e4','WORSHIP',DEVI(72,10,.55,False)+FIRE(40,160,.5)+FLOWERS(5)),
  ('#fff3b0','“CHOOSE A BOON”',DEVI(72,0,.6,False)+GODS(170,.35,3,30,40)),
  ('#e3f2fd','SHE VANISHES','<g opacity=".35">'+DEVI(72,0,.6,False)+'</g>'+T(72,150,'… Gaurī',12))]),
 ['<b>Worship.</b> Praised, she is honoured with flowers from Nandana, the garden of heaven, with perfumes and incense.',
  '<b>The offer.</b> Gracious, she says to the bowing gods: “Choose, all of you, whatever you desire from me.”',
  '<b>The answer.</b> “Nothing is left to do. But whenever we remember you, remove our dangers; and bless every mortal who praises you with these hymns.”',
  '<b>“Tathā!”</b> “So be it,” says Bhadrakālī, and vanishes.',
  '<b>A promise.</b> Medhas tells the king: “This is how she arose from the bodies of the gods. Hear now how she arose again, from the body of Gaurī, to slay Śumbha and Niśumbha.”'],
 ("She just vanishes?","She comes when called and goes when the work is done. That's the boon: she comes back whenever we remember her. The next time, as Medhas says, she comes from Gaurī."),
 'She comes when remembered: <b>that is the boon</b>.')
P=dict(n=9,ch=4,out='/home/claude/work/devim/Devi_Mahatmya_Part09.html',
 title='The Śakrādi Stuti · Part 9 · Devī Māhātmya series · srini.presents',
 desc='Devī Māhātmya, Part 9: chapter 4, the Śakrādi stuti. Indra and the gods praise the Goddess after Mahiṣa’s death: fortune in the homes of the good, Durgā the boat across the sea of life, “śūlena pāhi no devi”. Explained in plain English with illustrations.',
 h1='THE ŚAKRĀDI STUTI',series='DEVĪ MĀHĀTMYA · PART 9 · CHAPTER 4',tag='The gods’ hymn after victory · explained and illustrated',cover=cover,hymn=None,
 how='The whole hymn (ślokas 2–27) <b>in full for chanting</b>, then one page for each verse or group: the chant, a picture, what it says, the key words, one insight and the one thing to remember.',
 where=[f'In {plink(8)}, Mahiṣāsura changed shape again and again, and fell under the Goddess’s foot. The second carita’s battle is won.',
  'Chapter 4 is pure praise. Indra and the gods sing the <b>Śakrādi stuti</b>, one of the three great hymns of the Devī Māhātmya (with Brahmā’s hymn in chapter 1 and the Nārāyaṇī stuti in chapter 11). It is often chanted on its own.'],
 wtalk=("The battle's over. Why a whole chapter of praise?","Because the gods finally see who she is. And their hymn ends with a boon for us: she will come whenever she is remembered."),
 fulls=[('THE HYMN IN ONE GO','Chapter 4, ślokas 2–10',FV[:9]),('IN ONE GO · CONTINUED','Ślokas 11–19',FV[9:18]),('IN ONE GO · CONTINUED','Ślokas 20–27',FV[18:])],
 fulltoc='The Hymn in One Go',
 fullnote='Text as given in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in (42 ślokas in chapter 4); its numbering is followed. Editions number them slightly differently.',
 seq=V+[st],
 toc=[('THE HYMN · ŚLOKAS 2–27',V[:13]),('THE BOON',[V[13],('vanish','The Boon and the Promise')])],
 end=['<b>Praise (4.2–4).</b> The gods bow: she pervades the world by her own power; not even the trinity can describe her.',
  '<b>Who she is (4.5–11).</b> Fortune in good homes and ruin in bad; primal Nature; Svāhā and Svadhā; the Vedas; Medhā, Durgā, Śrī and Gaurī.',
  '<b>Her two faces (4.12–23).</b> Moon-like smile and terrible frown; even her killing purifies; compassion in the heart, ruthlessness in battle.',
  '<b>Protect us (4.24–27).</b> “Śūlena pāhi no devi”: with trident, sword, bell and bow, in every direction, with gentle and terrible forms.',
  '<b>The boon (4.28–42).</b> “Whenever remembered, remove our dangers.” She vanishes, to return from the body of Gaurī.'],
 endsub='Chapter 4 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",'Part 10: chapter 5, the third carita. The gods, oppressed by Śumbha and Niśumbha, go to the Himālaya and sing “yā devī sarvabhūteṣu”, the most famous hymn of the Goddess.'),
 endkey='<b>remember her, and she comes</b>.',
 foot='Study notes on the Devī Māhātmya, chapter 4 (Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa), the Śakrādi stuti. The hymn (ślokas 2–27) is given in full as in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in, whose numbering is followed. Meanings are our own plain-English renderings. Zappy and Prof. Bolt are fictional.')
